  {
    slug: 'credit-bureau-singapore-report-interpretation',
    title: 'Credit Bureau Singapore Report Interpretation: Understanding Credit Assessment Frameworks',
    excerpt: 'Credit Bureau Singapore reports contain comprehensive credit information affecting loan eligibility and pricing, requiring careful interpretation by borrowers.',
    content: `Credit Bureau Singapore (CBS) maintains comprehensive credit databases containing borrower payment histories, outstanding obligations, and credit utilisation patterns. Financial institutions access these reports during loan application assessments, making credit report understanding essential for Singapore borrowers seeking financing.

CBS credit reports include credit scores ranging from 1000 to 2000, with higher scores indicating stronger creditworthiness. Scores above 1800 typically qualify for competitive rates, while scores below 1600 may face challenges securing approval or favourable terms. Reports also detail payment histories across all credit facilities, showing payment patterns over 12-24 month periods.

Credit utilisation ratios represent outstanding balances relative to credit limits, with high utilisation potentially negatively impacting scores. Singapore borrowers should maintain utilisation below 30-40% of available credit limits, as high utilisation may indicate financial stress. Credit reports show utilisation across individual facilities and aggregate levels.

Credit inquiry history reveals recent loan applications, with multiple inquiries potentially indicating financial distress or credit-seeking behaviour. However, inquiries within short periods for similar loan types, such as mortgage shopping, may be treated as single inquiries. Singapore borrowers should limit unnecessary credit applications to avoid inquiry-related score impacts.

Default and delinquency records remain on credit reports for significant periods, typically 3-5 years, substantially impacting credit scores and loan eligibility. Settled defaults may be noted, though historical records still affect assessments. Bankruptcy records remain for extended periods, severely limiting credit access.

Singapore borrowers should regularly review credit reports, identifying inaccuracies and addressing issues proactively. CBS provides annual free reports, with subscription services enabling more frequent monitoring. Understanding credit report components enables borrowers to improve scores, address issues, and position themselves favourably for loan applications.`,
    author: 'Brilliance Advisory Team',
    date: '2023-02-28',
    category: 'Bank & Credit Behaviour',
    pillar: 'Bank & Credit Behaviour',
